      "lineNbr": 232,
      "author": {
        "id": 1006192
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-08-22T12:01:27Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e Confidentiality (don\u0027t disclose to unauthorized parties) is right, but \"no data … into external tools … or AI-assisted analyzers\" is broader than confidentiality and unenforceable — researchers routinely use scanners/static analyzers, many now AI-backed, and a report from someone who used one but found a real bug is still valuable.\n\nSharing security reports with AI tools allows the LLMs to learn about them and share the learning with other 3rd parties.\nGerrit is very much used in mission-critical projects and for developing the Android OS by Google and many other OEMs. Disclosing a severe security issue is a very serious matter, either directly or indirectly, by using insecure tools.\n\nIf maintaining the embargo makes it more expensive to investigate the security issue, then let it be for the sake of security and confidentiality.\nThe lock of a bank\u0027s safe is for sure more expensive than the lock of a bicycle, and there are reasons for that.\n\n\u003e Could we narrow this to non-disclosure (no public trackers / unauthorized sharing) rather than a ban on the reporter\u0027s private tooling?\n\nThe reporter is free to use an in-house LLM that does not share its domain knowledge with other 3rd parties.\nThere are plenty of open-source models that run on-premises locally.",
      "parentUuid": "1af73e7a_5c9404a2",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 231,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 232,
        "endChar": 27
      },
      "revId": "682f0cb1c0b5f4549564ede893622dfcdc42e949",
      "serverId": "173816e5-2b9a-37c3-8a2e-48639d4f1153"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "dcb1625e_c58abf1a",
        "filename": "Documentation/dev-processes.txt",
        "patchSetId": 6
      },
      "lineNbr": 243,
      "author": {
        "id": 1011323
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-08-22T11:26:16Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This \"not accepted\" line (and its twin on 255) is worth reconsidering. A large share of real vulnerabilities are found by reading code, not by weaponizing them — a missing permission check on a REST endpoint is a genuine vuln even without a working exploit. Requiring execution evidence before we\u0027ll even triage risks bouncing legitimate expert reports, whose likely next step is full public disclosure.\n\nCould we soften to \"code-analysis reports are welcome; maintainers may request a PoC when impact is unclear\" rather than a published hard rejection? (Also stated twice — 243 and 255.)",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 243,
        "startChar": 82,
        "endLine": 243,
        "endChar": 94
      },
      "revId": "682f0cb1c0b5f4549564ede893622dfcdc42e949",
      "serverId": "173816e5-2b9a-37c3-8a2e-48639d4f1153"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "da774de8_8f7a8842",
        "filename": "Documentation/dev-processes.txt",
        "patchSetId": 6
      },
      "lineNbr": 243,
      "author": {
        "id": 1006192
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-08-22T12:14:59Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Let me rephrase it: we will not accept just \"pure code-analysis\" without the end-to-end validation against the external impact.\n\nBy running code-analysis AI models, I could create hundreds of security reports based on code-paths: nowadays, that is cheap and costs no more than $100. Analysing the end-to-end impact of those reports takes days, if not weeks, of human work, which may cost tens of thousands of dollars, if not more.\n\nThe reporter who just ran their AI models and spent no more than $100 will then get the monetary reward from Google bug bounty, the Gerrit developers who worked night and day for triaging, understanding the end-to-end impact and fixing the code, spending tens of thousands of dollars, will get no reward.\n\nThe current model does not reward triaging or fixing them. We need to put more work into the reporting side to ensure that the work is more equally spread between reporters and developers fixing them.\n\nAlso, the Linux project ran into this issue, see:\nhttps://www.theregister.com/security/2026/05/18/linus-torvalds-says-ai-powered-bug-hunters-have-made-linux-security-mailing-list-almost-entirely-unmanageable/5241633\n\nBottom line: we want to *ensure* that whoever sends a security report has *done some work* to ensure that the report satisfies a set of requirements.\n\nMy team (4 people + me) has spent the past month working on security issues, and we cannot keep up with the continuous reports of more issues. The reality is that we cannot cope with the current flow of issues *IF* we do not offload the quality filter work to the reporter.\n\nLastly, the latest reports did not come from people who really know how Gerrit works, but rather from bottom-up code analysis, which may suggest there is an insecure path. By simply reading the Gerrit documentation, the report could have been withdrawn at its source, without wasting developers\u0027 time and money.",
      "parentUuid": "dcb1625e_c58abf1a",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 243,
        "startChar": 82,
        "endLine": 243,
        "endChar": 94
      },
      "revId": "682f0cb1c0b5f4549564ede893622dfcdc42e949",
      "serverId": "173816e5-2b9a-37c3-8a2e-48639d4f1153"
    },
